@DISTRO_ID@-base (0.1.5) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Depend on what stonking's systemd and udev recommend and a stock Ubuntu
    therefore installs, skipped here because Recommends are off: the TPM
    tools (systemd-cryptsetup, which carries systemd-cryptenroll;
    systemd-tpm, tpm-udev, libtss2-rc0t64), systemd-hwe-hwdb (Ubuntu's
    updated hardware database), libnss-systemd and linux-sysctl-defaults.
    TPM unlock of the disk is not turned on by this (docs/7 choice 47).

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:30:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-base (0.1.4) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Depend on fwupd and fwupd-signed, so every machine gets firmware
    updates: fwupd only recommends fwupd-signed, the Canonical-signed EFI
    program that applies UEFI capsule updates (BIOS/UEFI firmware), and
    Recommends are off here, so without it no system firmware update could
    be applied, with Secure Boot on or off.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-base (0.1.3) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Package description: drop the paragraph about an apt preferences file for
    snapd, which the system no longer has.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-base (0.1.2) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Add /usr/libexec/@DISTRO_ID@/loader-fallback and dpkg triggers on Ubuntu's
    signed GRUB and shim: on an EFI System Partition shared with another
    Ubuntu-family system (named in /etc/@DISTRO_ID@/previous-loader by the
    installer), the /EFI/ubuntu/grub.cfg stub tries this system's /boot and
    falls back to the previous system's GRUB, so deleting this system's
    partitions no longer leaves the machine at a GRUB prompt (Legion laptop,
    2026-10-12).

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 22:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-base (0.1.1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Unattended security updates: install /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/51@DISTRO_ID@-unattended-upgrades,
    which clears the ${distro_id} origins of Ubuntu's 50unattended-upgrades (lsb_release reads
    the diverted os-release and reports the distro's own name, so those origins matched nothing)
    and lists Ubuntu's security origins by their literal name.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Sat, 11 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-base (0.1.0) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Initial release: base system metapackage, os-release diversion,
    apt default (no Recommends), netplan renderer NetworkManager.

